no thats not right remember we are doing video posts with music and gradient/textured background you did not do that here

You are right. The command I gave only handled the content planning layer. It did not account for the visual style layer you are now using for the animated ambient posts.

From the help message, this pipeline looks like it works in two parts:

First, generate the month plan from the theme and weekly subthemes.

Second, apply or rerender with a style preset that controls the gradient or textured visual treatment.

So for May, the correct flow is this:

python acp.py init-month --theme "As God Has Said" --year 2026 --month 5 --subthemes "Before It Makes Sense, When It Feels Like Loss, Accepting the Position, Staying There"

Then validate it:

python acp.py validate --payload 2026-05/payload.json

Then generate the month:

python acp.py run-all --payload 2026-05/payload.json

Then check which visual presets exist for the gradient or textured look:

python acp.py list-presets

Then inspect the one you want:

python acp.py show-preset --style-preset "NAME_FROM_LIST_PRESETS"

Then rerender the month using that preset:

python acp.py rerender --payload 2026-05/payload.json --style-preset "NAME_FROM_LIST_PRESETS" --all

What I cannot confirm from the help screen alone is whether music and motion are already included inside the rerender step, or whether that happens in a later stage of your pipeline. The help text shown in your screenshot still describes this as creating daily publishable images, even though your workflow has evolved toward video posts. So the safest read is:

The May theme and subthemes go into init-month.

The gradient or textured visual treatment likely comes from list-presets, show-preset, and rerender.

The audio part may be either baked into the current renderer behind the scenes, or handled by a separate downstream step that is not visible in this help screen.

The next command you should run is:

python acp.py list-presets

Paste that output here and I’ll tell you exactly which full May command to use for the animated look.


